---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import GitignoreGenerator from '../../components/GitignoreGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Gitignore Generator" description="Generate a .gitignore file for your language or framework by combining common templates.">
  <GitignoreGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Combine multiple templates</h2>
  <p>Most real projects span more than one ecosystem — a Node backend with a Python data script, for example. Select multiple templates to combine them into a single .gitignore that covers every part of your stack.</p>

  <h2>Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Common templates for Node, Python, and general OS/editor files</li>
    <li>Combine multiple templates into one file</li>
    <li>Copy-ready output</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Select the template(s) matching your project's languages/tools.</li>
    <li>Copy the combined result.</li>
    <li>Save it as <code>.gitignore</code> in your project root.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Selecting "Node" adds entries like <code>node_modules/</code> and <code>.env</code>; adding "General" also ignores OS files like <code>.DS_Store</code> and editor folders like <code>.vscode/</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common .gitignore Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adding .gitignore after files are already tracked.</strong> Gitignore only prevents new files from being tracked — files already committed need to be explicitly removed with <code>git rm --cached</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring files that should be shared</strong>, like a <code>.env.example</code> template (as opposed to the real <code>.env</code>, which should be ignored).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Gitignore Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting up a new repository's .gitignore before the first commit</li>
    <li>Adding coverage for a second language/tool added mid-project</li>
    <li>Quickly checking what a standard .gitignore for a stack typically includes</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Gitignore FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What if I add .gitignore too late?</strong> Run <code>git rm -r --cached .</code> followed by <code>git add .</code> and a commit to remove already-tracked files that should now be ignored.</p>
</ToolLayout>
